.TH HIDREPORT 2
.SH NAME
hidreport \- HID report descriptors
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "hidreport.m";
hidreport := load Hidreport Hidreport->PATH;

Pgeneric, Pkeyboard, Pbutton: con ...;
Upointer, Umouse, Ukeyboard, Ux, Uy, Uwheel: con ...;
Input, Output, Feature: con iota;
Fconst, Fvar, Frel: con ...;

Field: adt {
    page:   int;
    usage:  int;
    umin:   int;
    umax:   int;
    lmin:   int;
    lmax:   int;
    off:    int;
    size:   int;
    count:  int;
    flags:  int;
    val:    fn(f: self ref Field, rep: array of byte, n: int): int;
};

Report: adt {
    id:     int;
    kind:   int;
    bits:   int;
    app:    int;
    fields: list of ref Field;
    find:   fn(r: self ref Report, page, usage: int): ref Field;
};

init:    fn();
parse:   fn(d: array of byte): (list of ref Report, string);
report:  fn(rl: list of ref Report, id, kind: int): ref Report;
kbd:     fn(r: ref Report): int;
keys:    fn(r: ref Report, rep: array of byte): (int, array of int);
ptr:     fn(r: ref Report): int;
mouse:   fn(r: ref Report, rep: array of byte): (int, int, int, int);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Hidreport
walks a HID report descriptor \- what a device sends when it will not
do the boot protocol \- and says where in each of its reports every
field is.
.PP
.B Init
must be called before anything else.
.PP
.BI parse( d )
walks the descriptor
.IR d .
It returns the reports in the order the descriptor declared them, and
a string, which is an error; when it is not nil the list is what was
understood before the error, which is usually still usable.
.PP
A
.B Report
is everything the descriptor put in the report with that
.B id
and of that
.B kind
\-
.BR Input ,
.B Output
or
.BR Feature .
An
.B id
of 0 means the device uses no report ids.
.B Bits
is how long the report's data is;
.B app
is the usage of the application collection it was declared in, with
the usage page in the top sixteen bits, or 0.
.PP
A
.B Field
is a run of
.B count
items, each
.B size
bits wide, starting
.B off
bits into the report's data.  The offset does not count a report id:
over GATT the id is in the Report Reference descriptor and not in the
value, and over USB the caller strips it, so what is left is the data
either way.  A variable field whose usages were named one by one
becomes one
.B Field
per item with
.B usage
set; a field given as a usage range \- the eight keyboard modifier
bits, a row of buttons \- stays one
.B Field
with
.B umin
and
.BR umax .
An array field, where the value selects a usage out of the range
rather than each item having one, has
.B usage
0 and
.B Fvar
clear.  Constant fields are padding: they take up room in the report
and are not in the list.
.PP
.IB f .val( rep ,\  n )
is the
.IR n th
item of
.IR f ,
read out of
.IR rep .
It is sign extended when the descriptor said the logical minimum goes
below zero.
.PP
.IB r .find( page ,\  usage )
is the field carrying that usage, matching either a field's own usage
or a range that covers it.
.PP
.BI report( rl ,\  id ,\  kind )
picks one report out of a parsed list.
.PP
.BI kbd( r )
says whether
.I r
looks like a keyboard, which means an array of key codes on the
keyboard page; a device with only modifier bits and no array is a set
of modifiers and not a keyboard.
.BI keys( r ,\  rep )
reads one: the modifier byte in the order the boot protocol puts it \-
bit 0 is Left Control \- and the key codes that are down, with the
empty slots and the rollover code left out.
.PP
.BI ptr( r )
says whether
.I r
has X and Y on the generic desktop page.
.BI mouse( r ,\  rep )
reads one: the movement, then the buttons in the order
.B /dev/pointer
wants them, then the wheel, which is 0 on a device that has none.  The
first three are what
.IR hidboot 's
.B mouse
returns and in the same order, so a caller that already handles the
boot protocol handles this.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/hidreport.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR hidboot (2),
.IR btfs (4),
.IR usbd (1)
.SH BUGS
Nothing here understands what a device is for beyond keyboards and
pointers, and nothing writes an output or a feature report; they are
parsed and then left.
